---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const p = (key: Parameters<typeof routePath>[1]) => routePath(locale, key);

const features = [
  { label: 'ADDRESS', title: 'Clinic and home visits', text: 'If you have a clinic, the address stays visible and home visits are added as a service area. The wrong setup costs you visibility on the map.' },
  { label: 'PROXIMITY', title: 'Neighborhood scale', text: 'Patients will come back again and again during treatment, so they look for somewhere close. We measure where you rank from different points around your neighborhood.' },
  { label: 'COMPLAINT', title: 'The patient’s language', text: 'Patients type “stiff neck” or “can’t lift my shoulder”. We build the pages on your website around these complaints.' },
  { label: 'TITLE', title: 'The right title', text: 'Professional titles are protected in many places, and a doctor’s title isn’t yours to use. We write your title correctly and avoid wording that looks like a diagnosis or promises a certain recovery.' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'adres', label: 'Clinic or home visits' },
  { id: 'yakinlik', label: 'Neighborhood proximity and repeat sessions' },
  { id: 'sikayet', label: 'Searches by complaint' },
  { id: 'kategori', label: 'Category and title' },
  { id: 'sayfa', label: 'Pages your website needs' },
  { id: 'yorum', label: 'When to ask for reviews' },
  { id: 'sinir', label: 'What we don’t write' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'I work both in a clinic and at patients’ homes. How should I set up the profile?',
    a: 'Set it up with the address, and add home visits separately as a service area. Hiding the address when patients come to you costs visibility in “near me” searches. On the website, open separate pages for the clinic and for home visits.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use the word “treatment” on my website?',
    a: 'You can when describing the method you apply. A physiotherapist’s scope of practice is set by the professional rules where you practice, and it differs by country and state. We write copy that doesn’t look like a diagnosis or promise a certain recovery, check the wording against those rules and publish it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does an online booking system affect rankings?',
    a: 'There’s no information that it affects rankings directly. The benefit is on the booking side: a patient who can’t reach you by phone books through the link on the profile. For a physiotherapist who can’t pick up the phone during working hours, that’s a real advantage.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I’m in a small city with few competitors. Do I need SEO?',
    a: 'Yes, though the job is smaller. Where competitors are few, the profile setup and a handful of good pages come first, and a long content program may not be needed. We tell you this in the free analysis, based on your area’s data.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When will I see results?',
    a: 'We can’t give you a timeframe; it depends on where your business starts, on the competition and on the channel. What we can tell you is the order of the work: we measure first, fix the root problems, then produce. You see what we did and how the measurement changed in your weekly report.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="industries/physio"
  title="SEO for Physiotherapists and Google Maps | Wiserrise"
  description="Local SEO for physiotherapy clinics and home physiotherapy: address and service area setup, complaint pages, measurement across your neighborhood."
  eyebrow="INDUSTRY · PHYSIOTHERAPISTS"
  h1="SEO for physiotherapists: show up for patients searching in your neighborhood."
  crumb="Physiotherapists"
  lede="SEO for physiotherapists means appearing in front of patients who live near you and type in their complaint. A patient types “slipped disc pain” or “physiotherapist near me” and looks for a place they can keep coming back to during treatment. We fix your profile’s address setup first, then the complaint language on your website."
  serviceName="Local SEO for Physiotherapists"
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Industries', href: p('industries') },
  ]}
  features={features}
  toc={toc}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { label: 'Local SEO', href: p('services/local-seo'), text: 'We fix your profile and your website together for your map ranking.' },
    { label: 'Content Production', href: p('services/content'), text: 'We write complaint and service pages in the patient’s own words.' },
    { label: 'SEO for Dentists', href: p('industries/dental'), text: 'How we work on emergency toothache searches and treatment pages.' },
    { label: 'SEO for Aesthetic Clinics', href: p('industries/aesthetics'), text: 'How we work in a field where decisions take long and price comes up all the time.' },
  ]}
  relatedHeading="Related pages"
  ctaLabel="Get a free analysis"
  ctaHref="/#contact"
>
  <h2 id="adres">Should you show your address?</h2>

  <p>
    Yes, if you have a clinic patients come to. Google recognizes two kinds of profile: businesses with an address that
    patients visit, and those that define only a service area without showing an address. A physiotherapist who has a
    clinic but hides the address loses part of their visibility in “physiotherapist near me” searches.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is the most important decision in the profile setup, and it’s a hassle to fix later. If there’s a clinic, the
    address stays visible and the home visits you offer are added separately as a service area. The website gets a separate
    page for each.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you only do home visits, a profile without an address is the right choice. Even with the address hidden, Google
    takes the profile’s registered address into account for ranking; we explain the details on the
    <a href={p('services/gbp')}>profile management page</a>. You can check your profile’s current setup with our
    <a href={p('tools/gbp-audit')}>free profile audit tool</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="yakinlik">Why do patients choose a physiotherapist close by?</h2>

  <p>
    Because they won’t come just once. For most patients physiotherapy doesn’t end in a single session, so they look for
    somewhere they can travel to and from for weeks. With an aching back, nobody wants to cross the city.
  </p>

  <p>
    That’s why competition in physiotherapy plays out at neighborhood scale. Someone typing “physiotherapist Headingley” or
    just “physiotherapist” sees you in the same list as a clinic a few streets away, and where the searcher stands changes
    the order. A single measurement taken at your clinic door won’t show you this picture.
  </p>

  <p>
    We take <a href={p('services/rank-tracking')}>measurement across your area</a>: where you rank from different points
    around your neighborhood. Work done without seeing the streets where you fall behind is done blind. You see the work
    we do every week in the report.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sikayet">What do patients type when they search?</h2>

  <p>
    Their complaint. Most physiotherapy websites describe the methods they use, such as manual therapy or dry needling.
    Patients, meanwhile, type things like:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>“slipped disc pain won’t go away”</li>
    <li>“stiff neck what to do”</li>
    <li>“can’t lift my shoulder”</li>
    <li>“knee exercises after surgery”</li>
    <li>“heel spur treatment”</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The gap between these two languages is the biggest content gap in the industry. A page that explains why the complaint
    happens and how physiotherapy deals with it answers the patient in their own words.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>A complaint page works nationwide, a service page works in your neighborhood.</strong> In a search for
      “slipped disc exercises” proximity has little effect, and you can rank across the country. In a search for
      “physiotherapist Southbank” proximity decides, so we meet the two with separate pages.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="kategori">Which category and title are right?</h2>

  <p>
    The primary category should be “Physiotherapist” or, depending on your type of practice, “Physical therapy clinic”.
    The list also has options that look similar, like “Massage therapist” and “Rehabilitation center”, and each one
    corresponds to a different cluster of searches. With the massage therapist category, it gets harder to appear in front
    of patients looking for physiotherapy.
  </p>

  <p>
    The title needs the same care. Professional titles are protected in many places, and which ones depend on where you
    practice. A physiotherapist using a title that belongs to a doctor, or a qualification they don’t hold, is both wrong
    and a problem under the health advertising rules. We write your title correctly
    on the profile and the website, which also makes it easier for Google to place you in the right category.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sayfa">Which pages should your website have?</h2>

  <p>This is the basic structure we build:</p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>Complaint pages: back, neck, shoulder, knee and sports injuries</li>
    <li>Service pages: physiotherapy at the clinic, physiotherapy at home, rehabilitation after surgery</li>
    <li>Physiotherapist credentials: education and certificates</li>
    <li>A page explaining what happens at the first appointment</li>
    <li>Structured data (schema) of the Physiotherapy type</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The first appointment page looks small, yet it’s one of the most useful pages. The biggest hesitation of a patient
    coming to physiotherapy for the first time is the unknown: what to wear, or what will happen in the first session. A
    clinic that answers these questions up front reduces the hesitation on the phone.
  </p>

  <p>
    To write and publish these pages we need to run the website, and if we can’t manage the site completely, we don’t take
    on the work. We explain how we write on the <a href={p('services/content')}>content production</a> page, and how we fit
    each page to a single search on the
    <a href={p('services/on-page-seo')} style="white-space: nowrap;">on-page SEO</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="yorum">When should you ask patients for a review?</h2>

  <p>
    Towards the end of treatment. Repeat sessions give you a natural point to ask: the same stage of treatment for every
    patient, one patient at a time, with no incentives. Asking only the patients you think are satisfied breaks Google’s
    rules, so we don’t pick. A review requested at the first session comes too early and feels forced. Health advertising
    rules can also limit how reviews are used; in Australia, for example, AHPRA restricts testimonials in health
    advertising, so check with your own regulator how the rules where you practice treat this.
  </p>

  <p>
    Don’t offer a discount or a free session in return for a review, because it breaks Google’s rules. When replying,
    don’t mention the patient’s complaint or treatment, because the reply is public. We explain the review request flow in
    the <a href={p('guides/more-reviews')}>guide to getting more Google reviews</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sinir">What don’t we write?</h2>

  <p>
    A physiotherapist’s scope of practice is set by the professional rules where you practice, and it differs by country
    and state. The health advertising rules come on top of this, and when you’re unsure, your own regulator or
    professional body has the final word. So we leave the following out of the content:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Wording that looks like a diagnosis</li>
    <li>Certain recovery, “guaranteed results” or treatment guarantees</li>
    <li>Before/after images and incentives like discounted session packages</li>
    <li>Using patient reviews and stories like advertising</li>
    <li>Superiority claims like “the best physiotherapist”</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Describing methods is fine: how manual therapy is applied, how a session goes. A page that gives a clear answer to the
    patient’s question is easy to write within these limits.
  </p>

  <p>
    We explain how the same rules work for <a href={p('industries/dental')}>dental clinics</a> and
    <a href={p('industries/aesthetics')}>aesthetic clinics</a> on separate pages.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <h2>Where do you <span class="highlight">rank</span> in your neighborhood?</h2>
    <p>
      The free analysis is a video we record for your business: where you stand today and what to start with first. It
      lands in your inbox within <span style="white-space: nowrap;">24 hours</span>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ContentLayout>
